examples.question_text:
For the polynomial graph shown, determine the sign of the leading coefficient and whether the degree is odd or even.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=9mm,y=9mm,every node/.style={text=black,font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\definecolor{curveblue}{HTML}{268CFF}
\draw[black,<->] (-3,0)--(3,0);
\draw[black,<->] (0,-2.6)--(0,2.2);
\path (3,0) node[right] {$x$} (0,2.2) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[curveblue,thick,<->,domain=-1.6:1.6,samples=100,smooth,variable=\x] plot ({\x},{1-0.5*(\x)^4});
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

examples.solution_text:
The leading term is $(-2x)(x)(x)=-2x^3$. The degree is odd: the left end rises and the right end falls.

Let $x=0$:
$y=(3)(2)(-1)=-6$.

Let $y=0$:
$0=(3-2x)(x+2)(x-1)$.
The $x$-intercepts are $(-2,0)$, $(1,0)$ and $(\frac32,0)$. Each factor has a distinct zero, so the curve crosses at all three intercepts.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=11mm,y=9mm,every node/.style={text=black,font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\definecolor{curveblue}{HTML}{268CFF}
\draw[black,<->] (-2.8,0)--(3,0);
\draw[black,<->] (0,-2.6)--(0,2.6);
\path (3,0) node[right] {$x$} (0,2.6) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[curveblue,thick,<->,domain=-2.34:2.24,samples=180,smooth,variable=\x] plot ({\x},{0.2*(3-2*\x)*(\x+2)*(\x-1)});
\fill[black] (-2,0) circle (1.3pt) (1,0) circle (1.3pt) (1.5,0) circle (1.3pt) (0,-1.2) circle (1.3pt);
\path (-2,0) node[below left] {$(-2,0)$} (1,0) node[below left] {$(1,0)$} (1.5,0) node[above right] {$(\frac32,0)$} (0,-1.2) node[left] {$(0,-6)$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
The sketch shows intercepts and end behaviour; the heights of the turning points are not labelled.

$\begin{aligned}y&=x^4-20x^2+64\\&=(x^2-4)(x^2-16)\\&=(x-2)(x+2)(x-4)(x+4)\end{aligned}$
The leading term is $x^4$, so both ends rise.

Let $x=0$: $y=64$.

Let $y=0$:
$0=(x-2)(x+2)(x-4)(x+4)$.
The zeroes are $-4,-2,2,4$. The curve crosses the $x$-axis at each zero.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=6mm,y=9mm,every node/.style={text=black,font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\definecolor{curveblue}{HTML}{268CFF}
\draw[black,<->] (-5,0)--(5,0);
\draw[black,<->] (0,-1.6)--(0,2.6);
\path (5,0) node[right] {$x$} (0,2.6) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[curveblue,thick,<->,domain=-4.48:4.48,samples=180,smooth,variable=\x] plot ({\x},{0.025*((\x)^4-20*(\x)^2+64)});
\fill[black] (-4,0) circle (1.3pt) (-2,0) circle (1.3pt) (2,0) circle (1.3pt) (4,0) circle (1.3pt) (0,1.6) circle (1.3pt);
\path (-4,0) node[below left] {$(-4,0)$} (-2,0) node[below right] {$(-2,0)$} (2,0) node[below left] {$(2,0)$} (4,0) node[below right] {$(4,0)$} (0,1.6) node[above right] {$(0,64)$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

$\begin{aligned}Q(x)&=x^2-x-6\\&=(x-3)(x+2)\end{aligned}$
Hence $P(x)Q(x)=(x+4)(x-3)(x+2)$.

The zeroes are $-4,-2,3$. The leading term is $x^3$, so the left end falls and the right end rises.

At $x=0$, $y=(4)(-3)(2)=-24$.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=7mm,y=9mm,every node/.style={text=black,font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\definecolor{curveblue}{HTML}{268CFF}
\draw[black,<->] (-5.5,0)--(4.5,0);
\draw[black,<->] (0,-3)--(0,2.8);
\path (4.5,0) node[right] {$x$} (0,2.8) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[curveblue,thick,<->,domain=-5:3.63,samples=180,smooth,variable=\x] plot ({\x},{0.1*(\x+4)*(\x-3)*(\x+2)});
\fill[black] (-4,0) circle (1.3pt) (-2,0) circle (1.3pt) (3,0) circle (1.3pt) (0,-2.4) circle (1.3pt);
\path (-4,0) node[above left] {$(-4,0)$} (-2,0) node[above right] {$(-2,0)$} (3,0) node[below right] {$(3,0)$} (0,-2.4) node[left] {$(0,-24)$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

examples.question_text:
The graph is a cubic with three distinct zeroes. Find its equation in factored form.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=9mm,y=9mm,every node/.style={text=black,font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\definecolor{curveblue}{HTML}{268CFF}
\draw[black,<->] (-3,0)--(4.8,0);
\draw[black,<->] (0,-2.8)--(0,2.8);
\path (4.8,0) node[right] {$x$} (0,2.8) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[curveblue,thick,<->,domain=-2.43:4.58,samples=180,smooth,variable=\x] plot ({\x},{-0.25*(\x+2)*(\x-1)*(\x-4)});
\fill[black] (-2,0) circle (1.3pt) (1,0) circle (1.3pt) (4,0) circle (1.3pt) (0,-2) circle (1.3pt);
\path (-2,0) node[below left] {$-2$} (1,0) node[above right] {$1$} (4,0) node[above right] {$4$} (0,-2) node[left] {$(0,-16)$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

examples.solution_text:
Take out the common factor $x$, then factorise the difference of squares:
$\begin{aligned}y&=x^3-36x\\&=x(x^2-36)\\&=x(x-6)(x+6)\end{aligned}$

The leading term is $x^3$, so the left end falls and the right end rises.

Let $x=0$: $y=0$.

Let $y=0$:
$0=x(x-6)(x+6)$.
By the null factor law, the zeroes are $0,6,-6$. The common factor $x$ gives the zero at the origin. All three zeroes are distinct, so the curve crosses at each intercept.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=4mm,y=9mm,every node/.style={text=black,font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\definecolor{curveblue}{HTML}{268CFF}
\draw[black,<->] (-8,0)--(8,0);
\draw[black,<->] (0,-2.8)--(0,2.8);
\path (8,0) node[right] {$x$} (0,2.8) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[curveblue,thick,<->,domain=-7.2:7.2,samples=180,smooth,variable=\x] plot ({\x},{0.02*\x*((\x)^2-36)});
\fill[black] (-6,0) circle (1.3pt) (0,0) circle (1.3pt) (6,0) circle (1.3pt);
\path (-6,0) node[below left] {$(-6,0)$} (0,0) node[above left] {$(0,0)$} (6,0) node[below right] {$(6,0)$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
